Recall that for a real or complex Banach space X with dual X*, the numerical radius of a bounded linear operator T : X → X is
  v(T) := sup{ |x*(T x)| : x ∈ X, x* ∈ X*, ‖x‖ = ‖x*‖ = x*(x) = 1 },
and the numerical index of X is the constant
  n(X) := inf{ v(T) : T ∈ L(X), ‖T‖ = 1 } = max{ k ≥ 0 : k‖T‖ ≤ v(T) for all T ∈ L(X) }.

Compute the exact value of n(X) for each of the following classical Banach spaces:

(a) X = C^m[0,1], the space of m-times continuously differentiable real (or complex) functions on [0,1], equipped with any of its usual norms (for instance ‖f‖ = max_{0 ≤ k ≤ m} sup_{t ∈ [0,1]} |f^(k)(t)| or ‖f‖ = ∑_{k=0}^{m} (1/k!) sup_{t ∈ [0,1]} |f^(k)(t)|);

(b) X = Lip(K), the Banach space of bounded Lipschitz scalar-valued functions on a complete metric space (K, d), endowed with the norm ‖f‖ = max{ ‖f‖_∞, sup_{x ≠ y} |f(x)−f(y)|/d(x,y) };

(c) X a Lorentz function space L_{p,q}(μ) (for 1 < p < ∞, 1 ≤ q ≤ ∞) or a Lorentz sequence space d(w,p);

(d) X an Orlicz function or sequence space L_φ equipped with the Luxemburg (or Orlicz) norm associated to an Orlicz function φ.

In each case the goal is an explicit determination of the numerical index in terms of the relevant parameters (m, the metric K, the indices p, q and weight w, or the Orlicz function φ).

